20	## The protection this replaces (state this in the PR body)
21	
22	Deleting the hard filter also deletes an incidental protection: the season tag was the only thing keeping a wool sweater out of a 30°C July outfit. So this plan does not merely delete it — **Task 4 replaces it with the measured equivalent**: `weatherRules` gains heat-based material exclusions, driven by the temperature we already fetch. A coarse month-derived label is swapped for a real reading of the day, which is the whole argument of the change.
23	
24	Task 4 also fixes a **pre-existing** dead end of the same family: `excludeMaterials` is a hard filter, so a closet whose only shoes are suede already returns zero outfits in the rain. Adding a heat list would multiply that risk. The fix is one rule applied to both — **a weather exclusion may narrow a required slot but may never empty it**; if it would, that slot is refilled without the exclusion.
25	
26	What remains a genuine trade-off: with a thin closet you can still get a mildly off-season piece, because ranking prefers rather than forbids. That is deliberate and consistent with `personalBand`'s existing stance — "better a slightly-off outfit than none" (`lib/generator/rules.ts`).
